## What it is

LBSM, Latent Behavioral Structure in Low-Dimensional Statistical Manifolds, is an experimental statistical machine learning project. It asks whether adaptive, autonomous, sequential systems show structured, low-dimensional organisation in their behaviour even though what we can observe of them is high-dimensional.

The working hypothesis is that the telemetry such systems produce sits on structured low-dimensional manifolds rather than anywhere in the full space.

## What it studies

- whether behavioural regimes cluster naturally in a latent space;
- whether adaptation, such as reinforcement-guided learning, shapes that geometry;
- whether behavioural drift shows up as the manifold moving;
- whether behaviour over time has a compact statistical description;
- whether any of this carries over from the synthetic setup to real adaptive systems.

Phase 1 uses reinforcement-guided synthetic agents because their regimes are known by construction. They are the first controlled test of the idea, not the limit of its scope.

## The work so far

Seven notebooks cover telemetry generation, manifold learning, HMM inference, online anomaly detection, reinforcement-learning behavioural evolution, manifold visualisation, and a robustness analysis over a 160-configuration grid. The exported datasets and a PDF report for each notebook are in the repository.

## What it is

Project Ouroboros is a modular wireless security platform on ESP32 hardware, built for defence, monitoring and controlled research. It is for education and research only, and the repository says so up front. The repository is archived.

It has two parts:

- **Blue Pill**, a defensive shield for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and sub-GHz. It does real-time intrusion detection, TinyML anomaly detection, automated mitigation, and shows spectrum waterfalls on a small display.
- **OuroMini**, a prototype built in 15 days for an expo. It scans Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and sub-GHz in real time, draws signal-strength bars, waterfalls and ghost traces on an OLED, logs to an SD card, and has a switch between a defensive and a research mode.

The firmware is modular, so each protocol can be updated on its own, and it ships as prebuilt images with a standard flashing process.

## What I did

I wrote 121 of the commits, from September 2025 to October 2026. A few others sent small commits: Harshitha Reddy, Kandukoori Tejaswini, Karedla Rupa Yeshvitha, Sai8705 and karthikkk.

## The firmware port

OuroMini's firmware is a port. The base is ESP32 Marauder by Just Call Me Koko (MIT licensed, credited in the repository), which did not support our board, a custom ESP32 design with an OLED, SD card and the radio modules from the architecture above. Getting it running on that board was my work. Comparing the repository with Marauder v1.8.8 shows what changed:

- **The main sketch was rewritten**, about 470 lines added and 210 removed. Scanning moved from a polling loop to an asynchronous scan driven by the Wi-Fi driver's "scan done" event, with retries and a full driver recovery when it keeps failing.
- **A web interface was added**, which upstream does not have: a dashboard served from the board itself over HTTP and WebSocket, plus the shared state it needs (the access point, station and probe lists).
- **Settings storage was reworked**, the display code was adapted to the OLED, and the build was configured for the board: PSRAM on, a lower free-memory floor and a slower channel-hop delay.
- The firmware is branded and versioned as its own thing, "ESP32 OuroMini", and says in its header that it is inspired by Marauder.

The code below is from the files I wrote for the port. The firmware is for education and research, as the repository says.

**Scanning that recovers.** A scan that fails is retried, then the Wi-Fi driver is restarted in place, and after three failures in a row the whole driver is torn down and rebuilt (`esp32_marauder.ino`).

```cpp
void startAsyncScan() {
    wifi_scan_config_t scanConf = {};
    scanConf.show_hidden = true;
    scanConf.scan_type = WIFI_SCAN_TYPE_ACTIVE;
    scanConf.scan_time.active.min = 100;
    scanConf.scan_time.active.max = 300;

    const int maxRetries = 3;
    esp_err_t err = ESP_FAIL;
    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < maxRetries; ++attempt) {
        err = esp_wifi_scan_start(&scanConf, false);   // non-blocking
        if (err == ESP_OK) break;

        if (err == ESP_ERR_WIFI_STATE) {
            // state conflict: light recovery, let the driver settle, retry
            esp_wifi_stop();
            vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(150));
            esp_wifi_start();
            vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(200));
            continue;
        }
        break;   // anything else: log it and stop spinning
    }

    if (err != ESP_OK && ++scanFailCount >= 3) {
        fullWifiRecovery();   // persistent failures: rebuild the driver
        scanFailCount = 0;
    }
}
```

**The full recovery** stops and deinitialises the driver, brings it back up in station-plus-access-point mode, and re-registers the scan-done handler, so the board heals itself without a reboot. The access point's password is blacked out (`esp32_marauder.ino`).

```cpp
void fullWifiRecovery() {
    esp_wifi_stop();           vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(200));
    esp_wifi_deinit();         vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(100));

    wifi_init_config_t cfg = WIFI_INIT_CONFIG_DEFAULT();
    if (esp_wifi_init(&cfg) != ESP_OK) return;
    esp_wifi_set_storage(WIFI_STORAGE_RAM);
    esp_wifi_set_mode(WIFI_MODE_APSTA);

    wifi_config_t ap_config = {};
    strncpy((char*)ap_config.ap.ssid, "OuroMini", sizeof(ap_config.ap.ssid) - 1);
    strncpy((char*)ap_config.ap.password, "████████", sizeof(ap_config.ap.password) - 1);
    ap_config.ap.max_connection = 4;
    ap_config.ap.authmode = WIFI_AUTH_WPA_WPA2_PSK;
    esp_wifi_set_config(WIFI_IF_AP, &ap_config);

    esp_wifi_start();
    // re-register the scan-done handler, unregistering a stale one first
    if (scan_done_handler_instance != NULL) {
        esp_event_handler_instance_unregister(WIFI_EVENT, WIFI_EVENT_SCAN_DONE, scan_done_handler_instance);
        scan_done_handler_instance = NULL;
    }
    esp_event_handler_instance_register(WIFI_EVENT, WIFI_EVENT_SCAN_DONE, &onScanDone, NULL, &scan_done_handler_instance);
}
```

**The dashboard link.** The board serves a WebSocket, and the dashboard sends JSON that is turned into commands for the board's own command line. Plain text is accepted too, which is handy from a terminal (`WebInterface.cpp`).

```cpp
ws->onEvent([this](AsyncWebSocket *server, AsyncWebSocketClient *client,
                   AwsEventType type, void *arg, uint8_t *data, size_t len) {
    if (type == WS_EVT_CONNECT) {
        updateClients();                        // send the current state to the new client
    } else if (type == WS_EVT_DATA) {
        AwsFrameInfo *info = (AwsFrameInfo*)arg;
        if (info->opcode != WS_TEXT) return;
        String msg = String((char*)data).substring(0, len);

        StaticJsonDocument<512> doc;
        if (!deserializeJson(doc, msg)) {
            const char *typeStr = doc["type"];
            if (typeStr && strcmp(typeStr, "exec") == 0 && doc.containsKey("cmd")) {
                String cmd = doc["cmd"].as<String>();
                if (onCommand) onCommand(cmd);
                DynamicJsonDocument ack(128);
                ack["term"] = String("Cmd Exec: ") + cmd;
                String out; serializeJson(ack, out);
                client->text(out);              // acknowledge to the dashboard
            }
        } else {
            msg.trim();                         // not JSON: treat it as a raw command line
            if (msg.length() && onCommand) onCommand(msg);
        }
    }
});
```

## What it was

PhishTrap was a phishing-simulation challenge for students, run with the Department of Cyber Security and IoT at Malla Reddy University. Participants played the attacker in a closed, ethical setting: they researched pre-built personas with open-source intelligence, wrote personalised phishing emails, set up look-alike pages, and then documented what their approach would have achieved.

## What I did

I coordinated the event as a student coordinator and built the simulation environment from scratch, using Kali Linux, Python and Flask. It was a sandbox with controlled mail delivery and logging, hosted look-alike pages, and scripts for traffic logging and the target-side behaviour, so each participant faced a realistic multi-target setup without touching anything real. Running it live meant coordinating the team and scoring in real time.

## Who else

Planned and run with B. Sree Divya, senior advisor and event coordinator, and Kanne Karthik, peer lead and OSINT strategist. The repository is archived.

## The Code

Excerpts from the lab I built: the levels, the script that brought it up, and the mailer. The mailer could only send to a short fixed list of made-up addresses, through a local mail catcher on the same machine, so nothing it sent could leave the lab. The persona addresses are blacked out with █.

**Starting the lab** (`phish.sh`).

```bash
#!/bin/bash
echo "Starting MailHog..."
MailHog > mailhog.log 2>&1 &
sleep 2
echo "MailHog web UI: http://localhost:8025"

echo "Starting Flask app at http://127.0.0.1:5000 ..."
python app.py
```

**The sandboxed mailer** (`app.py`). It only accepts a target by name from a fixed list, and the mail goes to a local test server on port 1025, so it never reaches a real inbox.

```python
targets = {
    "████": "████████@catchmail.local",
    "█████": "█████████████@catchmail.local",
    "███████": "████████████@catchmail.local",
}

if target_key in targets:
    to_email = targets[target_key]
    msg = EmailMessage()
    msg['Subject'] = subject
    msg['From'] = f"{sender} <no-reply@phishlab.io>"
    msg['To'] = to_email
    msg.set_content(body)
    with smtplib.SMTP('localhost', 1025) as smtp:   # local MailHog only
        smtp.send_message(msg)
```

**The levels** (`app.py`). Each level is a short chain of pages in the lab, and finishing the last page of a level shows its flag. The routes only move the player on to the next page. They do not store anything a player types, and the page templates are not shown here.

```python
# Level 1
@app.route('/level1', methods=['GET', 'POST'])
def level1():
    if request.method == 'POST':
        return redirect('/level1_flag')
    return render_template('level1.html')

# Level 2: two pages, then the flag
@app.route('/level2_slack', methods=['GET', 'POST'])
def level2_slack():
    if request.method == 'POST':
        return redirect('/level2_drive')
    return render_template('level2_slack.html')

@app.route('/level2_drive', methods=['GET', 'POST'])
def level2_drive():
    if request.method == 'POST':
        return redirect('/level2_flag')
    return render_template('level2_drive.html')

# Level 3: an invite page, a portal, then the flag
@app.route('/level3_invite', methods=['GET', 'POST'])
def level3_invite():
    if request.method == 'POST':
        return redirect('/level3_portal')
    return render_template('level3.html')
```

## What this is

[caelestia-shell-mango](https://github.com/evoziosk/caelestia-shell-mango) is the Caelestia desktop shell ported from Hyprland to the MangoWC compositor. It is maintained by Evozio, not by me. I use it on my own machines, and this page is about the fix I sent in, which was merged upstream on 19 August 2026.

## The problem

On MangoWC every drawer toggle (launcher, dashboard, session and the rest) did nothing at all. No crash and nothing on screen. The only trace was in Quickshell's log, where every toggle printed the same errors:

```
@modules/Shortcuts.qml[125:-1]: TypeError: Value is null and could not be converted to an object
@services/Hypr.qml[75:-1]: TypeError: Property 'map' of object function values() { [native code] } is not a function
```

## What I found

Two separate bugs:

1. `monitorNames()` in `Hypr.qml` called `monitors.values.map(...)`. On MangoWC `monitors` is a plain array, so `.values` without parentheses is the array method itself, and `.map()` on it throws.
2. The real cause of the silent no-op was in `Mango.qml`. `monitorFor(screen)` built a brand new object on every call, while `focusedMonitor` was a different, cached object. `Visibilities.qml` keeps a `Map` keyed by object identity, so the lookup behind every drawer toggle never matched and came back `undefined`.

## What I changed

The one-line fix for the first bug:

```diff
-        return monitors.values.map(e => e.name);
+        return monitors.map(e => e.name);
```

For the second, `monitorFor()` now returns the same cached `focusedMonitor` object instead of a new one each time:

```diff
     function monitorFor(screen): var {
-        // MangoWC doesn't have per-screen monitor info easily accessible via Wayland protocols
-        return {
-            name: focusedOutput,
-            id: 0,
-            focused: true,
-            lastIpcObject: {
-                specialWorkspace: { name: "" }
-            },
-            activeWorkspace: focusedWorkspace
-        };
+        // single-monitor-only, so return the same cached object as focusedMonitor
+        // rather than a fresh literal each call -- Visibilities.qml keys a Map by object
+        // identity, and a fresh object here would never match on lookup.
+        return root.focusedMonitor;
     }
```

A second commit on 16 August fixed a side effect of that change. A property holding an object literal was creating a binding loop with `focusedWorkspace`, so I turned `focusedMonitor` into a persistent object whose identity stays stable:

```qml
// Persistent object (not a property-var literal) so its identity stays stable across
// reads -- Visibilities.qml keys a Map by this object, and a fresh literal here would
// also form a binding loop with focusedWorkspace, which references this property back.
readonly property QtObject focusedMonitor: QtObject {
    readonly property string name: root.focusedOutput
    readonly property bool focused: true
    readonly property var activeWorkspace: root.focusedWorkspace
    // ...
}
```

## How I checked it

I rebuilt and reinstalled the shell, restarted it, and toggled every drawer repeatedly. The log went from throwing both errors on every toggle to completely clean, the process stayed alive and the drawers opened and closed correctly.

The change touched two files, 19 lines added and 20 removed, in two commits.
